(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-2-pharm-e2-mi-vignettes"]={"title":"Myocardial Ischemia Drugs — Clinical Vignettes 1","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 72-year-old man with exertional angina is admitted with two weeks of worsening breathlessness, orthopnea, leg swelling and bibasilar crackles. Blood pressure is 118/70 mmHg and heart rate is 84 beats per minute. Which antianginal is contraindicated in this condition?","choices":["Atenolol","Isosorbide dinitrate","Amlodipine","Sublingual nitroglycerin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Acute decompensated heart failure is a contraindication to a beta blocker, because its negative inotropic effect can push a congested patient into worse failure. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man has sudden tearing chest pain radiating to the back and ST-segment elevation. Blood pressure differs between the two arms, and imaging shows an aortic dissection. Which drug is contraindicated?","choices":["Metoprolol","Labetalol","Tenecteplase","Morphine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Aortic dissection is a contraindication to fibrinolysis, because clot lysis can lead to catastrophic bleeding. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 65-year-old man with stable angina has type 2 diabetes mellitus and a reduced ejection fraction of 35 percent. He takes aspirin and metoprolol. Which added drug may slow progression of his coronary disease?","choices":["An angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor","A long-acting nitrate","A dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker","A non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is used in coronary disease with diabetes or left ventricular systolic dysfunction and may prevent disease progression, though it does not relieve angina. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man has 40 minutes of chest pressure at rest. The electrocardiogram shows ST-segment depression without ST elevation, and the cardiac biomarker is elevated. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["ST-elevation myocardial infarction","Non-ST-elevation myocardial infarction","Unstable angina pectoris","Chronic stable angina pectoris"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. No ST elevation with a raised biochemical marker separates a non-ST-elevation infarction from unstable angina. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman started a new antianginal two weeks ago. She now has a throbbing headache, facial flushing and swelling of both ankles. She has no dyspnea or orthopnea, and her jugular venous pressure is normal. Which drug class best explains these findings?","choices":["A dihydropyridine","A beta blocker","A non-dihydropyridine","A long-acting nitrate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Headache, flushing and peripheral edema are the typical effects of the dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers, which dilate arterioles strongly. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 56-year-old man with an ST-elevation myocardial infarction was treated with streptokinase for a previous infarction years ago. He has no bleeding history. Which fibrinolytic should be avoided now?","choices":["Alteplase","Streptokinase","Reteplase","Tenecteplase"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Prior streptokinase exposure or a previous allergic reaction to it is a listed contraindication to using it again. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 54-year-old man with an acute coronary syndrome still has chest pain after aspirin. Blood pressure is 130/82 mmHg and he takes no phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitor. Which drug relieves his pain but does not improve survival?","choices":["Aspirin","Metoprolol","Nitroglycerin","Clopidogrel"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Nitrates give chest pain relief with no mortality benefit, starting sublingually and moving to an intravenous infusion in hospital. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman keeps her sublingual nitroglycerin tablets in a weekly pill organizer on the bathroom counter. Which storage instruction is correct?","choices":["Weekly organizer, away from sunlight","Original container, cool and dry place","Refrigerator door, in a sealed bag","Car glove box, within easy reach"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The tablets are stored in their original packaging in a cool, dry place so that they stay potent. The bathroom's moisture is the problem here. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man presents two hours after the onset of crushing chest pain. The electrocardiogram shows ST-segment elevation in the anterior leads. He has no bleeding history, no prior stroke and no recent surgery. Which drug restores coronary flow by dissolving the clot?","choices":["Enoxaparin","Clopidogrel","Alteplase","Nitroglycerin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A fibrinolytic activates plasminogen bound to fibrin, dissolving the clot in an early ST-elevation myocardial infarction with no contraindication. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man with stable angina takes atenolol. His exertional chest pain persists. Pulse is 64 beats per minute, and the electrocardiogram shows normal conduction. Which added drug carries the greatest danger of symptomatic bradycardia and heart block?","choices":["Amlodipine","Felodipine","Isosorbide mononitrate","Verapamil"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker also slows the sinoatrial and atrioventricular nodes, so adding it to a beta blocker risks bradycardia and block. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man is starting a beta blocker for stable angina. Which set of findings should be followed?","choices":["Potassium, creatinine, blood pressure","Heart rate, blood sugar, lipids","Platelet count, hemoglobin, bleeding","Liver enzymes, thyroid tests, weight"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Beta blockers slow the heart and can raise blood glucose and worsen lipid profiles, so all three are monitored. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man has an ST-elevation myocardial infarction two hours after onset. Blood pressure is 128/78 mmHg and heart rate is 82 beats per minute. He vomited blood and passed black stools this week, and a gastroscopy found a bleeding ulcer. Which drug class should be avoided?","choices":["A beta blocker","A nitrate","An opioid analgesic","A fibrinolytic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Active bleeding and serious recent gastrointestinal hemorrhage are fibrinolytic contraindications, because these drugs break down clots and raise bleeding risk. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man with ST-elevation myocardial infarction still rates his pain 9 out of 10 after repeated sublingual nitroglycerin. Blood pressure is 124/76 mmHg. Which drug is used for chest pain unresponsive to nitrates?","choices":["Metoprolol","Morphine","Clopidogrel","Enoxaparin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is the opioid analgesic used for pain unresponsive to nitrates in ST-elevation infarction, though its use is controversial. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with stable angina uses sublingual nitroglycerin as needed. He asks to start tadalafil for erectile dysfunction. What is the main danger of combining them?","choices":["Profound hypotension","Bradycardia with heart block","Loss of nitrate effect","Bronchospasm"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Nitrates raise cyclic guanosine monophosphate and phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitors block its breakdown, so vasodilation is exaggerated, risking hypotension, infarction or stroke. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 24-year-old college athlete has exertional chest pain and near-syncope. A systolic murmur grows louder with the Valsalva maneuver, and echocardiography shows asymmetric septal hypertrophy with outflow obstruction. Which drug class would be harmful?","choices":["A beta blocker","A non-dihydropyridine","A nitrate","An antiplatelet drug"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Obstructive cardiomyopathy is a listed nitrate contraindication, and aortic stenosis is the other structural one. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man is started on verapamil for exertional angina because a beta blocker was not tolerated. Which adverse effect should he be counseled about?","choices":["Vivid nightmares","Raised blood sugar","Bronchospasm","Constipation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Dizziness and constipation are the listed counseling points for calcium channel blockers, so he should expect and plan for constipation. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 53-year-old man's exertional angina resolves within minutes of a sublingual nitroglycerin tablet. Which effect most directly lowers the oxygen demand of his heart?","choices":["Beta-1 blockade slows the heart","Slowed atrioventricular node conduction","Blocked calcium entry into the heart","Venodilation lowers ventricular volume"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Nitrates generate nitric oxide, which relaxes vascular smooth muscle; venodilation lowers filling and wall tension, which reduces oxygen demand. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with stable angina and peripheral vascular disease is about to start a beta blocker. Which adverse effect is he most at risk of?","choices":["Worsened pain when walking","Constipation with hard stools","Swelling of both ankles","Throbbing headache on standing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Worsened claudication is a listed beta blocker adverse reaction, and peripheral vascular disease is a listed precaution for the class. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man wears a nitroglycerin patch around the clock. After several weeks his exertional angina returns despite the same patch. Which change best restores its effect?","choices":["A second patch at night","Daily aspirin added","A daily nitrate-free interval","Ointment around the clock"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Continuous exposure causes tachyphylaxis, so a patch-free interval, such as twelve hours on and twelve hours off, restores the response. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man with stable angina still has exertional attacks three times a week on metoprolol alone. Heart rate is 66 beats per minute, blood pressure is 140/84 mmHg, and conduction and ventricular function are normal. Which added drug best relieves his symptoms?","choices":["Verapamil","Felodipine","Enalapril","Clopidogrel"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. When a beta blocker alone fails, a dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker is added, because it lowers wall tension without slowing conduction. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man with stable angina has a history of hives and wheezing after taking aspirin. Which drug should replace aspirin to prevent acute coronary syndrome?","choices":["Enoxaparin","Isosorbide mononitrate","Metoprolol","Clopidogrel"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It is as effective as aspirin for secondary prevention and is recommended when a patient is allergic to aspirin. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 56-year-old man with exertional angina stopped his beta blocker because of vivid nightmares and fatigue. Heart rate is 74 beats per minute and blood pressure is 134/82 mmHg. He has no asthma, conduction disease or heart failure. Which drug should replace it?","choices":["Diltiazem","Carvedilol","Isosorbide dinitrate","Clopidogrel"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker is the initial therapy when a beta blocker is not tolerated, and he has no contraindication to it. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 22"}]};
